heart attacks during pregnancy are  increasing among women in the u.s. a  12-year study led by NYU School of  Medicine is out this morning it found  the risk of having a heart attack while  pregnant giving birth or during the two  months after delivery rose 25% from 2002  to 2014 four and a half percent of the  women who had heart attacks died dr.  Holly Anderson is a cardiologist at New  York Presbyterian while Cornell Medical  Center good morning great joining you on  so why are we seeing an increased number  of pregnant women having heart attacks  while they're pregnant and then  postpartum I'm not surprised at all  because when you look at the national  data heart disease deaths and young  women have been increasing since the  year 2000 so it makes sense that that  heart attacks are happening more in our  pregnant women as well what do you mean  by young women what's Adria talking  about we look at young women to be 29 to  45 this study looked at women 18 and  above who are pregnant between the year  2002 and 2014 25% increase is  significant so what do you attribute it  to well first of all pregnancy is a big  risk on the heart so when when a  pregnant women is significantly more  likely to have a heart attack than non  pregnant women because you have a bigger  blood volume you gain weight your heart  increases your heart rate increases your  blood pressure increases and right at  the time of delivery you have to prevent  bleeding so your body clamps down and  you become more likely to form blood  clots all predispose you to heart  attacks but I think what's happening in  our young women is that the risk factors  for heart disease are increasing in this  country high blood pressure high  cholesterol diabetes are all higher in  our younger women and I think stress  probably plays a factor as well so what  could women do who are pregnant please  I'd like all women to know that heart  disease is the number one cause of death  for women and it's increasing in young  women and it's a very preventable  disease  so know your risk factors and work to  decrease them blood pressure your weight  your sugar levels don't smoke get your  get your your sugar levels checked and  your cholesterol levels checked and your  blood pressure checked and work to get  it better you know eat right exercise  try to good good night's sleep  but talk to your doctor about your risk  factors it's really important to bring  this up when you're seeing your doctor  to emphasize it's a preventable  preventible very much doctor thanks so  much dr. Anderson
 

